// ============================================================================
// THE DATABASE BLUEPRINT — every fact this business stores, and how the facts
// connect to each other.
//
// WHAT IT IS
//   Not code that runs. It's a description, and two things are built from it:
//     1. The real database tables (via the migration files in migrations/).
//     2. The `prisma.customer.findMany(...)` style commands the server uses.
//   Change something here and you must create a migration, or the description
//   and the actual database drift apart.
//
// WHERE IT'S USED
//   server/src/index.ts is the only thing that reads the database.
//   client/src/types.ts is the client's hand-written echo of these shapes —
//   if you add a field the screens need, it has to be added there too.
//
// HOW IT ALL HANGS TOGETHER
//
//     Customer ──< Visit >── Locker
//                    │
//                    ├──── Bill ──< BillLineItem      (what they owe)
//                    │
//                    └──< Order ──< OrderItem         (what the kitchen makes)
//
//     Category ──< MenuItem        (the catalogue, standing apart)
//
//   A VISIT is the hub: one person, one locker, one stay. It has exactly one
//   BILL, and any number of kitchen ORDERS.
//
//   Note the menu is NOT connected to bills. When something is sold, its name,
//   price and tax rate are COPIED onto the charge as plain values. That's
//   deliberate: it means changing a price — or deleting an item entirely —
//   can never alter what an old receipt says.
//
// (Unfamiliar with tables, fields and migrations? See CODE-GUIDE.md.)
// ============================================================================
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// LOCAL-FIRST CHANGE (2026-08-19) — read this before touching an id or a
// foreign key anywhere below.
//
// Mississauga is getting its own server instead of sharing the one at the
// owner's house (see claude/sauna-pos-architecture-change-local-first.md in
// the project docs — not in this repo). Niagara Falls gets one too, next
// year. Each site's box can go offline and keep trading; it streams its
// database to the owner's house in the background instead of depending on it
// live. That only works if two sites can never hand out the same id to two
// different rows — which a per-site `1, 2, 3...` counter absolutely would.
//
// So FIVE models below switched from an auto-incrementing integer id to a
// UUID, and picked up a `siteId` column: User, Customer, Visit, Order, Bill.
// These are the models a site actually creates on its own and that the
// owner's house needs to tell apart by origin. Everything else — Locker,
// Table, Category, MenuItem, Settings, Override, and the two line-item
// tables (OrderItem, BillLineItem) — stays a plain integer, because nothing
// about this change requires it to be anything else:
//   - Locker and Table are fixed furniture, one set per building. Nothing
//     ever merges two sites' locker lists, so there's no collision to guard
//     against.
//   - Category, MenuItem and Settings are the catalogue/config, shared and
//     unscoped for now (see the implementation guide's open questions on
//     whether pricing should sync from the owner's house later).
//   - OrderItem and BillLineItem are always read and written through their
//     parent Order/Bill, never looked up or merged on their own — so only
//     the FOREIGN KEY pointing at that parent needs to change type, not
//     their own id.
//
// The database is also SQLite now, not Postgres — see the `datasource`
// block below. That's what makes a per-site box painless to deploy (one
// file, no separate database container) and lets Litestream stream it to
// the owner's house continuously.
//
// `siteId` is stamped automatically by a Prisma Client Extension in
// server/src/index.ts (the `$extends`/`query` block, not the older `$use`
// middleware API — that one is being phased out), from a required SITE_ID
// environment variable. It is never something a screen sends. See that file
// for the actual mechanism, and prisma/seed-users.ts for the one other place
// that has to repeat the same SITE_ID check by hand, because it opens its own
// separate PrismaClient rather than going through index.ts's wrapped one.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Turns this description into the typed commands the server writes.
generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

// Which database, and where. The address lives in server/.env, out of version
// control, because it contains the password.
//
// SQLite as of the local-first change above (was Postgres). DATABASE_URL is
// now a file path, e.g. `file:/data/prod.db` in production — see
// docker-compose.prod.yml and litestream.yml.
datasource db {
  provider = "sqlite"
  url      = env("DATABASE_URL")
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// FIXED LISTS OF CHOICES
//
// An "enum" is a field that may only ever hold one of these exact words. The
// database itself refuses anything else, so a typo can't quietly create a
// locker in a third gender pool or an order in a status nothing recognises.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The two halves of the menu. FOOD_DRINK is what the kitchen cooks — that
// alone decides whether selling something prints a ticket.
enum MenuGroup {
  FOOD_DRINK
  MERCH_SERVICE
}

// Used for both guests and lockers, because lockers are split into a men's
// pool and a women's pool and a guest may only use their own.
enum Gender {
  MALE
  FEMALE
}

// A locker is free, in use, or out of service.
enum LockerStatus {
  AVAILABLE
  OCCUPIED
  MAINTENANCE
}

// How a bill was settled. Only CASH and CARD can be chosen at checkout — the
// other two exist in the data but aren't offered as buttons.
enum PaymentMethod {
  CASH
  CARD
  GIFT_CARD
  VISIT_PASS
}

// What a Visit actually is. STAY is the normal thing: somebody in the building
// with a locker, a tab, and a check-out still to come. TAKEOUT is a counter
// sale to somebody who isn't staying — no locker, usually no profile, paid on
// the spot. Both live in the same table because they share everything that
// matters: a bill, charges, and kitchen tickets.
enum VisitKind {
  STAY
  TAKEOUT
}

// The four stages of a ticket, in order. Each board's three columns are the
// first three; reaching COMPLETE removes the card.
enum OrderStatus {
  QUEUED
  IN_PROGRESS
  READY
  COMPLETE
}

// WHICH BOARD a ticket appears on. A menu section is marked as one or the
// other, and every ticket it produces goes there and nowhere else — a burger
// and an orange juice are TWO cards, and neither board shows the other's
// items. That's the whole point: the cook and the bartender are two people in
// two places, each wanting a list of only their own work.
//
// An enum rather than an `isBar` switch. A switch says "one of two"; the day a
// third board appears (a juice counter, a spa desk) two switches could both be
// on and the database couldn't stop it. This can only ever hold one value,
// which is the fact being recorded.
enum Station {
  KITCHEN
  BAR
}

// ADMIN can see the takings, edit the menu, void charges and give refunds.
// STAFF can do everything else — check people in, take orders, take payment.
enum Role {
  ADMIN
  STAFF
}

// A staff login. Created by prisma/seed-users.ts, never through the app —
// there's no "add a user" screen.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: id is now a UUID, and `siteId` was added — decided 2026-08-19
// that staff accounts are separate per site, not shared. A login created at
// Mississauga only exists at Mississauga; someone working both locations
// needs an account at each.
//
// `username` is unique PER SITE, not globally — see the @@unique at the
// bottom of this model. Decided 2026-08-19, in the same pass. The old global
// constraint would have been fine while Mississauga was the only site, and
// then quietly broken: each site's own SQLite file would happily accept a
// "frontdesk" at both locations, and the two rows would only ever meet — and
// collide — once both sites' data landed together on the read-only standby at
// the owner's house. Doing it now costs one line; doing it after Niagara
// Falls is live means renaming somebody's login.
model User {
  id           String   @id @default(uuid())
  siteId       String
  // Lowercased before saving. Unique within a site (see @@unique below), so
  // Mississauga and Niagara Falls can each have their own "frontdesk".
  username     String
  displayName  String // the friendly name shown on screen
  role         Role     @default(STAFF)
  // NOT the passphrase. A scrambled version of it that can be checked against
  // but never turned back — so nobody, including an admin with full database
  // access, can read what anyone's passphrase actually is.
  passwordHash String
  createdAt    DateTime @default(now())

  // Both ends of a manager override. Prisma insists a relation is declared on
  // both models, and with TWO links to the same table it can't tell them apart
  // by type alone — hence the names, which must match Override exactly.
  approvedOverrides  Override[] @relation("OverrideApprover")
  requestedOverrides Override[] @relation("OverrideRequester")

  // LOCAL-FIRST (2026-08-19): one "frontdesk" per site, not one in the world.
  // Prisma turns this into a lookup key named `siteId_username` — which is
  // why the sign-in in server/src/index.ts now asks for
  // `where: { siteId_username: { siteId, username } }` instead of
  // `where: { username }`. That's the only shape of lookup this allows: a
  // username on its own is no longer enough to identify one row.
  @@unique([siteId, username])
}

// House-wide configuration. There is exactly ONE of these, always id 1 — a
// single scratchpad row rather than a list of anything.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: deliberately NOT given a siteId. Each site's box has its own
// single Settings row, id 1, same as before — there's nothing to tell apart
// by site because nothing about Settings is ever merged or compared across
// sites. Whether tax rate / cash discount should someday be centrally set
// from the owner's house is an open question (see the implementation guide),
// not something this migration answers.
model Settings {
  id                     Int   @id @default(1)
  // The default tax rate as a decimal: 0.13 means 13%. Only a STARTING POINT
  // for new menu items; it never changes anything already priced or sold.
  taxRate                Float @default(0.13)
  // Which menu item to charge automatically at check-in. The `?` means it can
  // be empty, in which case staff pick an admission by hand.
  //
  // Note this is a loose number, not a proper link — deleting that menu item
  // would leave this pointing at nothing.
  defaultAdmissionItemId Int?
  // THE CASH DISCOUNT. Dollars taken off the entry charge when a guest settles
  // up in cash — 5 means −$5.00. Zero switches the whole thing off, which is
  // also the default, so a fresh database never starts discounting on its own.
  //
  // Applied by the server at the moment of checkout, since that is the only
  // point at which anything knows how the guest is paying. See cashDiscountFor
  // in server/src/index.ts — it is the single place this rule is decided.
  cashDiscount           Float @default(0)
  // How big the entry charge has to be before the discount applies at all.
  // 15 means a $30 day rate qualifies but a $3 child admission doesn't, and
  // neither does a pass-funded visit, whose entry charge is $0.
  //
  // This is what keeps the entry charge from being driven negative: as long as
  // the minimum is larger than the discount, there is nothing to clamp.
  cashDiscountMinEntry   Float @default(0)
}

// A menu section: "Drinks", "Admission", "Massage".
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: unscoped — no siteId, id stays a plain integer. The catalogue
// is shared/duplicated per site rather than synced for now; see the
// implementation guide's open question on whether pricing should someday
// flow from the owner's house down to each site.
model Category {
  id          Int        @id @default(autoincrement())
  name        String     @unique // two categories can't share a name
  group       MenuGroup  @default(FOOD_DRINK)
  // Selling from here prints a ticket. Set automatically from `group` by the
  // server — Food & drinks means yes, everything else means no.
  //
  // The NAME is now narrower than the meaning: it answers "does this print a
  // ticket at all", not "does it go to the kitchen". Which board is the
  // separate question below. Renaming it would mean dropping and re-adding a
  // column on a till with real trading history, and every category would come
  // back as `false` until someone re-saved it by hand — i.e. every ticket in
  // the building silently stops printing. Not worth a better name.
  isKitchen   Boolean    @default(false)
  // WHICH BOARD, on the sections that print at all. Chosen by an admin on the
  // Menu screen. Meaningless, and ignored, on a section that prints nothing —
  // which is why a towel category carries an inert KITCHEN.
  //
  // Defaults to KITCHEN, and that default is what makes every category already
  // in the database correct without touching a single row: today every ticket
  // goes to the kitchen, and after this migration every ticket still does,
  // until somebody deliberately moves a section to the bar.
  station     Station    @default(KITCHEN)
  // These are entry charges. Picking one at the till REPLACES the guest's
  // existing entry charge rather than adding to their tab.
  isAdmission Boolean    @default(false)
  items       MenuItem[] // the items inside this section
}

// One sellable thing. The catalogue only — see the note at the top about why
// nothing here is linked to what's actually on a bill.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: unscoped, same reasoning as Category above.
model MenuItem {
  id           Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  categoryId   Int
  category     Category @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id])
  name         String
  price        Float // before tax
  description  String? // shown as a tooltip at the till
  // This item's own tax rate, as a decimal. Copied onto each charge at the
  // moment of sale, which is what allows one bill to mix 13%, 5% and 0%.
  taxRate      Float    @default(0.13)
  // The photo, stored as a (long) piece of text rather than a file — see the
  // shrinking code in client/src/MenuPage.tsx. Means no upload folder to back
  // up separately, at the cost of a chunky database row.
  imageData    String?
  // The "86 it" switch. Hidden items keep everything but vanish from the till.
  available    Boolean  @default(true)
  // Does selling this print a ticket for the kitchen? A bottled water is a
  // drink — same category, same tax — but nobody has to make it, so a ticket
  // for it is noise on the board. Switching this off is the exception.
  //
  // It can only ever turn tickets OFF. The category still decides whether the
  // kitchen is involved at all, so a massage can't opt IN by setting this.
  //
  // Defaults to true, deliberately. Get it wrong that way and a ticket prints
  // for something that needed none — visible, mildly annoying, fixed in
  // seconds. Default it to false and a guest's food order silently never
  // reaches the kitchen while they sit waiting. Fail the noisy way.
  sendsToKitchen Boolean @default(true)
  // THE TWO PASS FIELDS — opposites, and the easiest thing here to confuse:
  //   visitCredits — buying this GRANTS this many passes (a 10-pack: 10)
  //   redeemsPass  — choosing this SPENDS one of the guest's passes
  // One is the pack you buy; the other is the entry you get with it. Never set
  // both on the same item.
  visitCredits Int      @default(0)
  redeemsPass  Boolean  @default(false)
  // A DISCOUNT, not something you sell. Null on every ordinary item.
  //   "FIXED"   — takes a set number of dollars off
  //   "PERCENT" — takes a share off whatever is already on the tab
  // `price` stays 0 on these. Storing a negative price instead would mean
  // special-casing every place that shows what something costs — the till
  // tile, the "with tax" line in the editor, the receipt.
  discountKind  String?
  // For FIXED, dollars off: 5 means −$5.00.
  // For PERCENT, the rate: 20 means 20% off.
  discountValue Float   @default(0)
}

// A guest. The `?` fields are optional — only a name and gender are required,
// so the desk can sign someone up in seconds.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: id is now a UUID, and `siteId` was added — a customer signed
// up at Mississauga and one signed up at Niagara Falls must never collide
// once both sites' data lands at the owner's house. Every FK that pointed at
// Customer.id (Visit.customerId, Visit.passUsedCustomerId) changed to match.
model Customer {
  id               String    @id @default(uuid())
  siteId           String
  firstName        String
  lastName         String
  // Required, because it decides which locker pool they can use. The server
  // refuses to change it while they're mid-visit, since they'd be holding a
  // locker from the wrong one.
  gender           Gender
  phone            String?
  email            String?
  dateOfBirth      DateTime?
  address          String?
  // Allergies, warnings, preferences. Shown as a red pill at the till AND on
  // kitchen tickets, so it's the field that actually gets read.
  notes            String?
  // How many prepaid entries they have banked. Goes UP when they buy a pack
  // (immediately), DOWN when a pass-funded visit is checked out.
  visitPassBalance Int       @default(0)
  createdAt        DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt        DateTime  @updatedAt // set automatically on every change
  // Stays they made themselves. Named because there are now TWO links between
  // a customer and a visit and Prisma can't tell them apart by type alone —
  // the name is metadata only, it doesn't change anything stored.
  visits           Visit[]   @relation("VisitGuest")
  // Stays somebody ELSE made, paid for out of this customer's pass balance.
  sponsoredVisits  Visit[]   @relation("PassSponsor")
}

// A physical locker in the building. Created once by prisma/seed.ts (120 of
// them) and then just changes status as guests come and go.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: unscoped, id stays a plain integer. A locker is fixed
// furniture in one specific building — there is no scenario where two
// sites' locker lists need to merge, so there's no collision to guard
// against. See the note at the top of this file.
model Locker {
  id     Int          @id @default(autoincrement())
  number String
  gender Gender
  status LockerStatus @default(AVAILABLE)
  // Why this locker is out of service — "lock broken", "door won't shut".
  // Optional: staff can flag one without explaining. Cleared automatically
  // when it returns to service, so it never describes a working locker.
  maintenanceNote String?
  visits Visit[]
}

// ONE PERSON'S STAY — the hub everything else hangs off. Created at check-in,
// closed at check-out.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: id is now a UUID, and `siteId` was added, for the same reason
// as Customer above — a visit opened at one site must never collide with one
// opened at another once both land at the owner's house. `lockerId` stays an
// Int FK (Locker didn't change); `customerId` and `passUsedCustomerId` became
// String FKs (Customer did).
model Visit {
  id            String    @id @default(uuid())
  siteId        String
  // STAY or TAKEOUT. Defaulting to STAY is what makes every visit already in
  // the database correct without touching a single row.
  kind          VisitKind @default(STAY)
  // Both of these are now optional, and the `?` is the whole feature. A takeout
  // order has nobody signed in and no locker held. Every existing visit still
  // has both — nothing is being taken away, the rule is just being relaxed.
  customerId    String?
  customer      Customer? @relation("VisitGuest", fields: [customerId], references: [id])
  lockerId      Int?
  locker        Locker?   @relation(fields: [lockerId], references: [id])
  // WHOSE PASS PAID FOR THIS. Null means the guest used their own, or didn't
  // use one at all. Deliberately separate from customerId above: the person
  // staying and the person paying are different questions, and a member
  // bringing a friend is exactly when they differ.
  //
  // The balance still isn't touched until check-out — this only records whose
  // it will be. See the sponsor guard in index.ts for why that matters: a
  // sponsor can otherwise be committed to more guests than they have passes.
  passUsedCustomerId String?
  passUsedCustomer   Customer? @relation("PassSponsor", fields: [passUsedCustomerId], references: [id])
  // The ticket number called out at the counter — 1, 2, 3, starting again each
  // morning. Only takeout has one; a staying guest is found by locker instead.
  takeoutNumber Int?
  // Optional, and purely so the counter can shout something friendlier than a
  // number. "Dave", "blue jacket". Never used to identify anyone.
  takeoutName   String?
  checkInAt   DateTime  @default(now())
  // Empty means they're still in the building. This one field is what every
  // "who's here now" query in the app looks at.
  checkOutAt  DateTime?
  // This stay's entry is being paid with a prepaid pass rather than money. The
  // balance isn't actually reduced until check-out.
  redeemsPass Boolean   @default(false)
  bill        Bill? // exactly one tab (see the @unique on Bill.visitId)
  orders      Order[]
}

// A ticket — one card on the Kitchen board or the Bar board.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: id is now a UUID, and `siteId` was added, same reasoning as
// Visit/Bill. `visitId` became a String FK (Visit did); `tableId` stays an
// Int FK (Table didn't change).
model Order {
  id        String      @id @default(uuid())
  siteId    String
  visitId   String
  visit     Visit       @relation(fields: [visitId], references: [id])
  status    OrderStatus @default(QUEUED)
  // WHICH BOARD THIS CARD IS ON. Copied off the category when the ticket is
  // created and then frozen — the same trick BillLineItem.taxRate plays, for
  // the same reason.
  //
  // Frozen matters: an admin moving "Drinks" from the kitchen to the bar
  // must not yank a tea off the cook's board halfway through making it. The
  // card stays where it was printed; the NEXT tea goes to the bar.
  //
  // Defaults to KITCHEN so every ticket already on the board — including ones
  // being cooked while the migration runs — stays exactly where it is.
  station   Station     @default(KITCHEN)
  createdAt DateTime    @default(now()) // drives the "waiting 6 min" clock
  items     OrderItem[]
  // WHERE TO TAKE IT, when the guest is sitting in the lounge rather than
  // waiting at their locker. Optional — most orders have none, and the cook
  // falls back to the locker number as before.
  //
  // It hangs off the ORDER, not the visit, because staff choose it per order:
  // a guest can eat at one table and order again from another. That's also why
  // a second order to a DIFFERENT table starts a new ticket instead of joining
  // the existing one — one card, one destination.
  //
  // Takeout never has one. There's nobody sitting anywhere.
  tableId   Int?
  table     Table?      @relation(fields: [tableId], references: [id])
}

// One thing to make. Note there's no price here — the money side lives on the
// bill; this is purely the cooking instruction.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: id stays a plain integer, and there's no siteId here. An
// OrderItem is never looked up or merged on its own — it's always read and
// written through its parent Order, which already carries the collision-safe
// id and the siteId. Only the foreign key pointing at that parent changed
// type, to match Order.id.
model OrderItem {
  id       Int     @id @default(autoincrement())
  orderId  String
  order    Order   @relation(fields: [orderId], references: [id])
  // Matched against BillLineItem.description BY TEXT when a charge is voided —
  // there's no id linking the two.
  name     String
  note     String? // "extra hot", "no onions"
  // A pulled item isn't deleted from a ticket already being cooked — it's
  // flagged, shown struck through in red, and the cook dismisses it once
  // they've seen it.
  canceled Boolean @default(false)
}

// THE TAB for one visit. Opened at check-in, closed at check-out.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: id is now a UUID, and `siteId` was added, same reasoning as
// Visit. `visitId` became a String FK (Visit did) but keeps its `@unique` —
// still exactly one bill per visit, the type just changed.
model Bill {
  id            String         @id @default(uuid())
  siteId        String
  // `@unique` here is what enforces "one bill per visit" — the database itself
  // refuses to let a visit have two.
  visitId       String         @unique
  visit         Visit          @relation(fields: [visitId], references: [id])
  lineItems     BillLineItem[]
  // The house rate as it stood at check-in, kept as a record. NOT what the
  // maths uses — that's the rate on each charge below.
  taxRate       Float          @default(0.13)
  // All empty until check-out. Once `paidAt` is set the bill is closed:
  // charges can no longer be voided, and undoing it means a refund.
  paymentMethod PaymentMethod?
  paidAt        DateTime?
  // A refund is a STAMP, not a deletion. The charges all stay, so a reprinted
  // receipt still shows what was sold and that the money went back.
  refundedAt    DateTime?
  refundReason  String?
  createdAt     DateTime       @default(now())
}

// ONE CHARGE on a tab. Three teas are three of these, not one with a quantity —
// the screens group them for display, but the data stays one row per unit.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: id stays a plain integer, and there's no siteId here — same
// reasoning as OrderItem above. Only `billId`, the foreign key pointing at
// Bill, changed type.
model BillLineItem {
  id                  Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  billId              String
  bill                Bill     @relation(fields: [billId], references: [id])
  // Plain text copied from the menu item at the moment of sale, NOT a link to
  // it. This is why deleting an item or changing its price can never alter an
  // old receipt. It's also what kitchen items are matched against by name.
  description         String
  amount              Float // before tax
  // WHAT IT COST BEFORE STAFF CHANGED IT. Null on almost every charge — it's
  // only filled in when someone adjusts a price at checkout, comping a drink
  // or knocking something down for a regular.
  //
  // Two rules keep it honest, both enforced in the price-override endpoint:
  //   - written only the FIRST time a charge is adjusted, so $6 → $3 → $1
  //     still remembers $6 rather than drifting to $3
  //   - cleared again if the price is put back to what it was, so an undo
  //     leaves no trace of a change that no longer exists
  //
  // Nothing computes money from this. It exists so "what did we give away"
  // has an answer, and so the Checkout screen can show "was $6.00" — without
  // it an adjusted charge is indistinguishable from one that was always
  // priced that way, and an admin's comp leaves no record anywhere at all.
  originalAmount      Float?
  // THE RATE THAT ACTUALLY COUNTS. Frozen here at the moment of sale, so a
  // receipt always shows the tax charged on the day. Note the default is 0,
  // not 0.13 — anything saved without a rate sells tax-free.
  taxRate             Float    @default(0)
  // This is the entry fee. There's at most one per bill, it can be swapped but
  // not voided, and it's marked with a * on printed receipts.
  isAdmission         Boolean  @default(false)
  // How many passes this sale added to the guest's balance. Kept so voiding
  // the charge can take exactly those passes back off again.
  visitCreditsGranted Int      @default(0)
  createdAt           DateTime @default(now())
}

// A dining table in the lounge. Deliberately not a Locker: no gender, no guest,
// no bill. Anything ordered at a table goes on that guest's locker tab, so this
// model exists only to answer "is there somewhere to sit".
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: unscoped, same reasoning as Locker — fixed furniture in one
// building, nothing ever merges two sites' table lists.
model Table {
  id     Int         @id @default(autoincrement())
  number String
  // How many people it takes. Optional — leave it null and the screen simply
  // won't show a seat count.
  seats  Int?
  status TableStatus @default(AVAILABLE)
  // When it was marked occupied. Nothing enforces that a table gets cleared,
  // so this is what lets the screen show "occupied 6h 40m" and make a
  // forgotten table obvious. Cleared whenever the table stops being occupied.
  occupiedSince   DateTime?
  // Why it's out of use — "broken chair", "spill". Optional, and cleared when
  // the table returns to service so it never describes a working table.
  maintenanceNote String?
  // Kitchen tickets being run out to this table. The only thing that has ever
  // pointed at a Table — so removing one is no longer a plain delete, and the
  // count endpoint has to reckon with it.
  orders          Order[]
}

enum TableStatus {
  AVAILABLE
  OCCUPIED
  MAINTENANCE
}

// ONE MANAGER APPROVAL. A staff member hit something only an admin may do, a
// manager typed their password, and this row is the permission slip that came
// out. It's a row rather than a signed blob for two reasons: the audit trail
// falls out of it for free, and "already used" is a column the server can
// stamp, which is what makes single-use enforceable at all.
//
// ⚠️ KNOWN LIMIT, chosen deliberately: the token is NOT tied to the action it
//    was approved for. `action` is recorded for the log but never checked, and
//    `scope` comes from the client. So a PAGE approval for "Open Reports" will
//    also satisfy any other admin endpoint until it expires. Tightening that
//    means storing a capability here and checking it in requireAdmin.
//
// LOCAL-FIRST: unscoped, id stays a plain integer — an approval only ever
// matters on the site it happened at, and it's never looked up across sites.
// `approvedById` and `requestedById` changed type to String, because they
// point at User, which did change.
model Override {
  id            Int      @id @default(autoincrement())
  // The secret the client sends back in the X-Override header.
  token         String   @unique
  // What the manager was told they were approving. Shown in the log.
  action        String
  // ACTION = one use, minutes. PAGE = reusable while a screen is open.
  scope         String   @default("ACTION")
  approvedById  String
  approvedBy    User     @relation("OverrideApprover", fields: [approvedById], references: [id])
  requestedById String
  requestedBy   User     @relation("OverrideRequester", fields: [requestedById], references: [id])
  createdAt     DateTime @default(now())
  expiresAt     DateTime
  // Null until spent. THIS COLUMN IS the single-use rule.
  usedAt        DateTime?
}
